<p>British Prime Minister Keir Starmer faced a deepening revolt within the governing Labour Party after Health Secretary Wes Streeting resigned and said he had lost confidence in Starmer’s leadership, while potential rivals including Greater Manchester Mayor Andy Burnham and former deputy prime minister Angela Rayner positioned themselves for a possible leadership race. Starmer has not resigned, and allies said no formal leadership contest had been triggered. The rebellion followed Labour’s poor performance in local and regional elections held last week, in which the party lost votes to Reform UK on the right and the Greens on the left. The Conversation reported local council results showing Reform UK with 1,453 seats, Labour with 1,068, the Liberal Democrats with 844, the Conservatives with 801 and the Greens with 587; it also cited BBC analysis estimating national vote shares of Reform 26%, Greens 18%, Conservatives 17%, Labour 17% and Liberal Democrats 16%. Political uncertainty also hit UK financial markets: Politico reported that the pound had its worst run against the dollar in 18 months, the stock market fell and government borrowing costs reached multi-year highs as investors reassessed UK risk. The Business Times reported that British stocks, bonds and the pound fell on political uncertainty and inflation concerns.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/DCxeAe0jMRhrSa2bCsPGRmNaAKBiyztyn-Qa7N1d-tk14qLgKLfgoojlRkljFhoALeAKhxo4ZE7usVe-XXrV7YiujZf1wVezo2d6zNJTD1CQXEwxqCz5KPeUHCe9bY-l2cBua47CgNXdppMrCEy41yzjrNZVcWFGrqHWGrQDc24CiFnInT2N86Wwlyw360urDEKXHQ' alt='Members of the news media outside 10 Downing Street after days of turmoil in the Labour Party over Prime Minister Keir Starmer’s leadership.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Parliamentary split: Brecorder reported that more than 80 Labour MPs had urged Starmer to quit, while more than 100 Labour lawmakers had called for him to stay and four junior ministers had resigned.</li><li>Public ratings: YouGov polling cited by The Guardian found 11% of Britons viewed Starmer as a good or great prime minister, while nearly 60% rated him poor or terrible.</li><li>Policy disputes: HuffPost UK listed anger over scrapping winter fuel payments for 10 million pensioners, rows over gifts and hospitality, and U-turns on the two-child benefit cap, digital ID and farmers’ inheritance tax among factors that damaged Starmer’s standing.</li></ul><blockquote>Where we need vision, we have a vacuum. Where we need direction, we have drift. - Wes Streeting</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Steve Reed: The Guardian described the housing secretary as a close Starmer ally and a founding member of Labour Together, the network that helped bring Starmer to power. (<a href='https://www.theguardian.com/politics/2026/may/16/keir-starmer-labour-leadership-talk-burnham-streeting'>The Guardian</a>)</li><li>Wes Streeting: In his resignation letter, Streeting argued Starmer would not lead Labour into the next general election and said the party’s debate about what comes next needed a broad field. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/2000335/uk-health-minister-resigns-as-starmer-faces-party-revolt'>Dawn</a>, <a href='https://www.brecorder.com/news/40421216/uk-minister-quits-ahead-of-possible-challenge-to-starmer'>Brecorder</a>)</li><li>Tim Bale: The Queen Mary University of London politics professor said Labour appeared to have concluded that Starmer would need to go reasonably quickly and be replaced by someone who could connect with the public. (<a href='https://www.independent.com.mt/articles/2026-05-15/world-news/UK-Labour-government-faces-weeks-of-uncertainty-over-the-prime-minister-s-future-6736289757'>The Malta Independent</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.politico.eu/article/keir-starmer-uk-labour-leadership-turmoil-economy-pressures-pound-slump-stock-market/'>Starmer drama has UK markets reliving their Truss nightmare</a> - politico.eu</li><li><a href='https://www.